register(ArgRound);
define
    arDepth=0;
    arSrc=7;
    arOther=41;
    arA code={
        ++arDepth;
        arMine = arDepth;
        if arDepth < 3;     arB(arOther);
        print "  A depth" arMine " sees argument =" argument " local =" arMine:;
        };
    arB code={
        arA(arSrc);
        };
    arRun code={
        arA(arSrc);
        };
    ;
arRun();
print "ARGROUND SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

argRoundT -- TRY-AND-BUY B.  A -> B -> A, with a LOCAL and an ARGUMENT each
carried across the nested call.

arA is entered three times through two different actions.  Each activation
writes its own local (arMine = its depth) and reads BOTH its local and its
`argument` AFTER the round trip has returned.  Every activation of arA is
passed arSrc = 7; the intervening arB is passed arOther = 41, so a frame that
leaks hands back 41 and the row says so by value rather than by absence.

    A depth 3 ... argument = 7 local = 3
    A depth 2 ... argument = 7 local = 2
    A depth 1 ... argument = 7 local = 1

WHAT EACH COLUMN DISCRIMINATES, and neither is redundant:

  the LOCAL column   descending 3/2/1 means each activation kept its own.  All
                     three reading 3 is the trample -- an inner activation's
                     value handed back to the outer one, which is the defect
                     the save/restore bracket exists to prevent (K2x's shape,
                     one road over).

  the ARGUMENT column  7 at every depth means each activation kept the field it
                     was called with.  A 41 anywhere is arB's argument reaching
                     arA -- the mutual-recursion leak K6c pins on the other
                     road, and the reason the intervening call is passed a
                     DIFFERENT value on purpose.

⚠ 41 IS THE ANTI-VACUITY VALUE.  Had arB been passed arSrc too, a completely
broken channel that handed every callee the same node would print 7 three
times and pass.  The two values must differ or the argument column asserts
nothing.
